TWO NEW REUSABLE TOOLS WERE WRITTEN FOR THIS GROUP (both in tools/, not in work/):
  * tools/netlogin.py -- logs in to a device over TELNET or SSH and prints EVERY byte of the
    login exchange, banner included, then runs commands.  It is the network twin of the
    serial `conlogin.py`.  It was needed because this case must prove the telnet server by
    a real authenticated session, and because T47199's subject is a message that exists
    only in a login's byte stream.  No third-party modules: telnet is a raw socket with
    minimal IAC handling, ssh is the system client driven through a pty.
  * tools/tcpflow.py -- summarises the TCP conversations in a `tcpdump -w` pcap in pure
    Python: one row per flow, the flags seen in each direction, and a handshake verdict of
    ESTABLISHED / REFUSED (SYN answered by RST) / NO-ANSWER.  Every other decoder in tools/
    is application-specific, and none of them can tell "refused" from "never attempted" --
    which is exactly the discrimination an off/on/removed arc rests on.
  Both are catalogued in tools/README.md in the same commit as this case.

ONE EXISTING TOOL WAS CORRECTED, WITH THE TRANSCRIPT THAT JUSTIFIES IT:
  * tools/console.py `ADVISORY` gained `Telnet is insecure and deprecated`.  See STEP 2:
    `service telnet ip` prints `% Warning: Telnet is insecure and deprecated. Please use
    SSH.` and SUCCEEDS.  `cfg.py` read the `% ` line as a rejection and abandoned the rest
    of the batch (exit 2) -- the documented silent-truncation family.  The first run of
    STEP 2 is preserved verbatim in work/t1-on.log, and work/t1-verify.log shows the
    command had taken effect.  console.py's own rule is "add an entry only with a
    transcript showing the command took effect"; that transcript is those two files.
  * tools/netlogin.py was corrected mid-case: a refused AW+ login prints `Login incorrect`
    and then re-presents `<host> login:`, and the first version matched the login prompt
    before the failure string, grading a real refusal as UNKNOWN (see STEP 5).  The
    refusal itself was never in doubt -- the device's words are in work/t3-telnet-badcred.log
    -- but the tool now tests the failure pattern FIRST and treats a re-presented `login:`
    after the password as FAIL.

