    ------------------------------------------------------------
    Telnet server                      : Enabled
    Protocol                           : IPv4
    Port                               : 23
    awplus>show system
    System Status                                           Mon Oct 19 22:46:04 2026
    ...

The leading bytes `..... ..#..'...` are the telnet IAC option negotiation, which netlogin.py
refuses; after that the protocol is cleartext, as telnet is.  This is the TCP/23 session the
case asks to be shown in a capture.

Third instrument, the DUT's own log:
    2026 Oct 19 22:46:03 daemon.notice awplus telnetd[30356]: Connection from 10.38.22.33
    received.
DUT 22:46:03 UTC + 13 h + the measured 15 s skew = box 11:46:18 NZDT, which is inside the
capture window -- the three instruments are describing the same session.
STEP 3 VERDICT: PASS.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 4 -- CONTROL: the server accepts the connection but REFUSES a bad credential
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Designed expectation: with the server on, a credential the DUT does not hold must be
refused AT THE APPLICATION, not at TCP.  This separates "the server is listening" from
"anything can get in", and it is the control that the ON leg needs.
A NON-EXISTENT username was used rather than a wrong password for `manager`, deliberately:
AW+ locks an account after 5 failed logins with a timer that restarts on each attempt, and
swi_a on this very bench is already lost to that (queue, "NOTICE (process)").  A failure
against an unknown user cannot count against `manager`.
The credential used is the campaign's worthless one, `testing123`.

    tools/tb tool tb277 ckmgmt netlogin --proto telnet --host 10.38.22.34 --user ckbogus
        --password testing123 --login-timeout 20 --expect fail

    === telnet 10.38.22.34:23 as ckbogus ===
    --- bytes before the username prompt ---
    awplus login:
    --- bytes between username and password prompt ---
    Password:
    --- bytes after the password was sent (THE LOGIN BANNER) ---

    Login incorrect

    awplus login:
    LOGIN RESULT: UNKNOWN  proto=telnet  user=ckbogus  seconds=2.5

The DEVICE's answer is unambiguous: `Login incorrect`, then a fresh `login:` prompt.
The `UNKNOWN` was the TOOL's grading defect, fixed in-case (see TOOLS above); it is reported
here rather than quietly re-run, and the device bytes are the evidence either way.

On the wire, the same capture as STEP 3:

    flow 10.38.22.33:57714 <-> 10.38.22.34:23  packets=31  duration=2.463s
         => ESTABLISHED+DATA
       10.38.22.33:57714 -> 10.38.22.34:23  pkts=17  payload=52 B
            flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, SYN}
       10.38.22.34:23 -> 10.38.22.33:57714  pkts=14  payload=94 B
            flags={ACK, FIN.ACK, PSH.ACK, PSH.ACK.URG, SYN.ACK}
    payload 10.38.22.34:23 -> 10.38.22.33:57714 (94 B):
       awplus login: Password:
       Login incorrect

       awplus login:

94 bytes against the 2045 of the accepted session: the TCP connection is ESTABLISHED (so the
server is genuinely listening) and the application refused.  Exactly the discrimination the
case needs.

Third instrument, the DUT's own log:
    2026 Oct 19 22:46:15 daemon.notice awplus telnetd[30403]: Connection from 10.38.22.33
      received.
    2026 Oct 19 22:46:15 authpriv.notice awplus login: pam_unix(vty0:auth): check pass;
      user unknown
    2026 Oct 19 22:46:15 authpriv.notice awplus login: pam_unix(vty0:auth): authentication
      failure; logname= uid=0 euid=0 tty=/dev/pts/0 ruser= rhost=10.38.22.33
    2026 Oct 19 22:46:18 authpriv.notice awplus login: FAILED LOGIN 1 FROM 10.38.22.33 FOR
      (unknown), User not known to the underlying authentication module
`FAILED LOGIN 1 ... FOR (unknown)` -- the counter is against the unknown user, not against
`manager`.  That was the point of choosing a non-existent username.
STEP 4 VERDICT: PASS.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 5 -- THE "(IPv4)" IN THE TITLE IS A REAL, INDEPENDENT CONTROL
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This is the step the case title actually names, and it is the one a `show` command cannot
grade.  Designed expectation: the IPv4 and IPv6 halves of the telnet server are controlled
separately, and turning IPv4 OFF must stop IPv4 connections EVEN THOUGH the server as a
whole still reads Enabled.

5a. Add IPv6 (11:47:40, work/t4-v6on.log) -- the advisory fix now lets the batch continue:

    awplus(config)# service telnet ipv6
    % Warning: Telnet is insecure and deprecated. Please use SSH.
    awplus(config)#
        (advisory, not an error -- continuing): % Warning: Telnet is insecure and deprecated.
    awplus# show telnet
    Telnet server : Enabled / Protocol : IPv4,IPv6 / Port : 23
    awplus# show running-config | include telnet
    service telnet

5b. Remove the IPv4 half only, leaving IPv6 (11:48:10, work/t5-v6only.log):

    awplus(config)# no service telnet ip
    awplus# show telnet
    Telnet Server Configuration
    ------------------------------------------------------------
    Telnet server                      : Enabled
    Protocol                           : IPv6
    Port                               : 23
    awplus# show running-config | include telnet
    service telnet
    no service telnet ip

    tools/tb tool tb277 ckmgmt netlogin --proto telnet --host 10.38.22.34 --user manager
        --password friend --login-timeout 10
      -> TRANSPORT FAILED: telnet 10.38.22.34:23 -- [Errno 111] Connection refused  (exit 4)

    on the wire (t33378-v6only.pcap):
    flow 10.38.22.33:57716 <-> 10.38.22.34:23  packets=2  => REFUSED (SYN answered by RST)
       10.38.22.33:57716 -> 10.38.22.34:23  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={SYN}
       10.38.22.34:23 -> 10.38.22.33:57716  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={RST.ACK}

  *** `show telnet` says Enabled.  IPv4 TCP/23 is refused at the same instant. ***
  This is precisely the trap platforms/x960.md §5 warns about, and it is here a CORRECT
  behaviour rather than a defect -- but it means the only honest grade of "Telnet Server
  (IPv4)" is the one taken on the wire.  Had this case been graded from `show telnet`, the
  IPv4 half could have been off and the case would still have "passed".

5c. Put IPv4 back and take IPv6 away again (11:48:4x, work/t6-v4back.log):

    awplus(config)# service telnet ip
    awplus(config)# no service telnet ipv6
    awplus# show telnet
    Telnet server : Enabled / Protocol : IPv4 / Port : 23
    awplus# show running-config | include telnet
    service telnet
    no service telnet ipv6

    tools/tb tool tb277 ckmgmt netlogin --proto telnet --host 10.38.22.34 --user manager
        --password friend --login-timeout 15 --cmd "show telnet"
      -> LOGIN RESULT: USER  proto=telnet  user=manager  seconds=0.2
         Telnet server : Enabled / Protocol : IPv4 / Port : 23
    on the wire (t33378-v6only.pcap, same window):
    flow 10.38.22.33:57718 <-> 10.38.22.34:23  packets=49  duration=0.812s
         => ESTABLISHED+DATA then RST
       10.38.22.33:57718 -> 10.38.22.34:23  pkts=27  payload=83 B
       10.38.22.34:23 -> 10.38.22.33:57718  pkts=22  payload=519 B

The IPv4 service came back under the same stimulus that had just been refused: same client,
same address, same port, one configuration line apart.  No IPv6 address is configured on the
DUT's SVIs, so the IPv6 half was not exercised on the wire -- NOT MEASURED, and the IPv6
listener's existence is asserted here only from `show telnet`.  The IPv4 half, which is what
this case is about, is measured on the wire in both directions.
STEP 5 VERDICT: PASS.

